WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.327
In part one, we said that a class is a blueprint,

00:00:03.327 --> 00:00:06.540
and that every object made from it has its own data.

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:11.855
But when you write a class in Python, where does that data actually go?

00:00:11.855 --> 00:00:16.050
And what is this word `self`, which appears in every method?

00:00:16.833 --> 00:00:21.273
Welcome to Lecture 7, part 2: defining a class.

00:00:21.623 --> 00:00:23.586
We'll write the `Circle` class,

00:00:23.586 --> 00:00:27.258
watch what Python does in memory when it builds an object,

00:00:27.258 --> 00:00:29.663
and see exactly what `self` refers to.

00:00:30.433 --> 00:00:33.887
A class definition starts with the keyword `class`,

00:00:33.887 --> 00:00:37.273
and the class name, followed by an indented block.

00:00:37.623 --> 00:00:42.474
Class names are written in capitalized words, like `Circle`, or `BankAccount`.

00:00:42.474 --> 00:00:45.583
That's different from function and variable names.

00:00:45.933 --> 00:00:50.168
Inside the block goes a special method called the initializer.

00:00:50.168 --> 00:00:51.739
Its name is `__init__`,

00:00:51.739 --> 00:00:54.812
with two underscores before it and two after.

00:00:54.812 --> 00:00:58.773
Python calls it automatically, to set up every new object.

00:00:59.123 --> 00:01:00.242
One small note.

00:01:00.242 --> 00:01:04.718
The slides write method names like `getArea`, in camel case.

00:01:04.718 --> 00:01:10.163
We follow Python's standard style, so we write `get_area`, in lower case.

00:01:10.933 --> 00:01:13.013
Here's the `Circle` class.

00:01:13.363 --> 00:01:16.454
The `__init__` method takes a `radius`,

00:01:16.454 --> 00:01:18.515
with a default value of 1,

00:01:18.515 --> 00:01:21.843
and runs one line: `self.radius = radius`.

00:01:22.193 --> 00:01:27.925
Then come three methods: `get_perimeter`, `get_area`, and `set_radius`.

00:01:27.925 --> 00:01:31.153
Each one reads or changes `self.radius`.

00:01:31.503 --> 00:01:35.241
Notice that every method, including `__init__`,

00:01:35.241 --> 00:01:38.103
has a first parameter called `self`.

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:42.538
To create an object, you call the class like a function.

00:01:42.538 --> 00:01:45.980
This call is the constructor, and it does two things.

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:51.268
First, it creates a new, empty object of the class in memory.

00:01:51.268 --> 00:01:56.610
Second, it calls the `__init__` method, to set up that new object.

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:03.580
The constructor's arguments match the parameters of the `__init__` method, leaving out `self`.

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:05.884
Since `radius` has a default of 1,

00:02:05.884 --> 00:02:09.610
`Circle()` with no argument makes a circle of radius 1.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:14.160
Its perimeter is 2π, and its area is π.

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:17.942
`Circle(2)` makes a circle of radius 2.

00:02:17.942 --> 00:02:24.102
And we can also set the data field directly: `circle_one.radius = 10`.

00:02:24.102 --> 00:02:25.950
Now the area is 100π.

00:02:26.734 --> 00:02:28.876
Now let's slow right down,

00:02:28.876 --> 00:02:33.574
and follow `c = Circle(5)` in memory, one step at a time.

00:02:33.924 --> 00:02:37.971
Step 1. Python creates an empty `Circle` object.

00:02:37.971 --> 00:02:40.164
It has no data fields yet.

00:02:40.514 --> 00:02:43.064
Step 2. Python calls `__init__`,

00:02:43.064 --> 00:02:46.651
and a new frame appears, with two parameters.

00:02:46.651 --> 00:02:49.201
`self` refers to the new object.

00:02:49.201 --> 00:02:51.194
And `radius` refers to 5.

00:02:51.544 --> 00:02:55.417
Step 3. The line `self.radius = radius` runs.

00:02:55.417 --> 00:02:59.205
`self.radius` means the data field `radius`,

00:02:59.205 --> 00:03:02.304
of the object that `self` refers to.

00:03:02.654 --> 00:03:08.054
So the object now has a data field, `radius`, equal to 5.

00:03:08.654 --> 00:03:09.647
Look closely.

00:03:09.647 --> 00:03:12.856
There are two things called `radius` here.

00:03:12.856 --> 00:03:15.378
The parameter lives in the frame.

00:03:15.378 --> 00:03:18.052
The data field lives in the object.

00:03:18.052 --> 00:03:20.574
They just happen to share a name.

00:03:21.174 --> 00:03:23.208
Step 4. `__init__` returns,

00:03:23.208 --> 00:03:28.785
and its frame is thrown away, together with the names `self` and `radius`.

00:03:28.785 --> 00:03:31.574
But the object stays, with its field.

00:03:31.924 --> 00:03:36.364
Step 5. The value of `Circle(5)` is the new object,

00:03:36.364 --> 00:03:39.324
and the name `c` now refers to it.

00:03:40.101 --> 00:03:42.791
So `self` is the object being worked on.

00:03:42.791 --> 00:03:46.355
In `__init__`, it's the object that was just created.

00:03:46.355 --> 00:03:50.861
In any other method, it's the object that the method was called on.

00:03:51.461 --> 00:03:53.673
When you write `c.get_area()`

00:03:53.673 --> 00:03:57.639
Python looks up `get_area` in `c`'s class, `Circle`.

00:03:57.639 --> 00:04:01.301
Then it calls it with `c` as the first argument.

00:04:01.651 --> 00:04:03.496
A new frame appears,

00:04:03.496 --> 00:04:09.216
and the `self` in that frame points at the same object as `c`.

00:04:09.216 --> 00:04:11.245
So `self.radius` is 5,

00:04:11.245 --> 00:04:13.091
and the area is 25π.

00:04:13.441 --> 00:04:18.252
So `c.get_area()` is short for `Circle.get_area(c)`.

00:04:18.252 --> 00:04:23.803
That's why every method needs `self` as its first parameter,

00:04:23.803 --> 00:04:27.041
and why you never pass it yourself.

00:04:27.641 --> 00:04:29.380
You can even check it.

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:32.937
This little class has one method, `who_am_I`,

00:04:32.937 --> 00:04:34.993
which just returns `self`.

00:04:34.993 --> 00:04:37.681
And `p.who_am_I() is p` is `True`.

00:04:38.031 --> 00:04:40.242
By the way, `self` isn't a keyword.

00:04:40.242 --> 00:04:45.106
It's only the name that every Python programmer uses for the first parameter.

00:04:45.106 --> 00:04:45.991
So use it too.

00:04:46.768 --> 00:04:48.172
Time to predict.

00:04:48.172 --> 00:04:53.789
We make two circles: `c1` with radius 3, and `c2` with radius 4.

00:04:53.789 --> 00:04:56.685
Then we call `c2.set_radius(10)`.

00:04:56.685 --> 00:04:58.089
Pause the video.

00:04:58.089 --> 00:05:01.073
Which object does `self` refer to?

00:05:01.073 --> 00:05:04.408
And what are the two radii afterwards?

00:05:04.958 --> 00:05:08.379
`self` refers to the object before the dot,

00:05:08.379 --> 00:05:10.210
the one `c2` refers to.

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:12.438
So only that object changes.

00:05:12.788 --> 00:05:15.259
`c1`'s radius is still 3,

00:05:15.259 --> 00:05:18.028
and `c2`'s radius is now 10.

00:05:18.801 --> 00:05:21.801
Here's the mistake almost everyone makes once.

00:05:22.151 --> 00:05:25.773
In `__init__`, you write `radius = radius`,

00:05:25.773 --> 00:05:27.711
and forget the `self.`.

00:05:28.061 --> 00:05:33.112
That line just assigns the parameter to itself, inside the frame.

00:05:33.112 --> 00:05:35.676
Nothing is created in the object.

00:05:35.676 --> 00:05:39.872
And when `__init__` returns, the frame is thrown away,

00:05:39.872 --> 00:05:41.581
and the value is lost.

00:05:42.181 --> 00:05:46.901
Data fields are always created, and read, through `self.`.

00:05:47.668 --> 00:05:50.845
A data field is also called an instance variable,

00:05:50.845 --> 00:05:53.503
because every instance has its own value.

00:05:53.503 --> 00:05:56.226
And a method is called an instance method,

00:05:56.226 --> 00:05:59.468
because it works on the instance it was called on.

00:05:59.818 --> 00:06:02.262
Once an instance variable is created,

00:06:02.262 --> 00:06:05.698
every method of the class can use it through `self`.

00:06:06.048 --> 00:06:10.459
But a plain variable assigned inside a method is a local variable,

00:06:10.459 --> 00:06:12.197
just like in any function.

00:06:12.197 --> 00:06:14.268
It lives in the method's frame,

00:06:14.268 --> 00:06:16.808
and it's gone when the method returns.

00:06:17.158 --> 00:06:21.477
In this `Scope` class, `x` and `y` are instance variables,

00:06:21.477 --> 00:06:24.158
but `z` is local to the method `m1`.

00:06:24.508 --> 00:06:28.133
Notice that `m2` calls `m1` with `self.m1`.

00:06:28.133 --> 00:06:33.782
Inside a class, one method calls another method of the same object,

00:06:33.782 --> 00:06:35.468
also through `self`.

00:06:35.818 --> 00:06:37.657
So after `s.m2()`

00:06:37.657 --> 00:06:38.847
`s.y` is 2.

00:06:38.847 --> 00:06:42.417
But `s.z` is an `AttributeError`.

00:06:42.417 --> 00:06:45.338
The object never had a `z`.

00:06:46.135 --> 00:06:48.100
Let's put it together.

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:51.048
This program makes three circles,

00:06:51.048 --> 00:06:53.459
with radius 1, 25, and 125,

00:06:53.459 --> 00:06:55.335
and prints each area.

00:06:55.685 --> 00:06:59.119
Then it changes `circle2`'s radius to 100,

00:06:59.119 --> 00:07:01.245
and prints its area again.

00:07:01.595 --> 00:07:06.355
Why doesn't that change the areas of `circle1` and `circle3`?

00:07:06.905 --> 00:07:10.377
Because each object has its own `radius`.

00:07:10.377 --> 00:07:13.002
`get_area` reads `self.radius`,

00:07:13.002 --> 00:07:17.745
and `self` is whichever object the method was called on.

00:07:18.535 --> 00:07:22.535
Finally, three broken programs, from the slides.

00:07:22.885 --> 00:07:23.626
Program 1.

00:07:23.626 --> 00:07:26.146
`__init__` needs an argument, `i`,

00:07:26.146 --> 00:07:28.815
but we call `A()` with no arguments.

00:07:28.815 --> 00:07:30.371
That's a `TypeError`.

00:07:30.371 --> 00:07:36.005
The constructor's arguments must match `__init__`'s parameters after `self`.

00:07:36.355 --> 00:07:37.217
Program 2.

00:07:37.217 --> 00:07:42.647
A method named after the class is not an initializer in Python,

00:07:42.647 --> 00:07:45.318
although it is in Java and C++.

00:07:45.318 --> 00:07:46.956
Only `__init__` is.

00:07:46.956 --> 00:07:51.955
And `radius = 3` makes a local variable, not a data field.

00:07:52.305 --> 00:07:53.265
Program 3.

00:07:53.265 --> 00:07:55.857
`set_radius` has no `self`.

00:07:55.857 --> 00:08:00.945
So `a.set_radius(5)` passes two arguments, `a` and 5,

00:08:00.945 --> 00:08:03.825
but the method only takes one.

00:08:04.602 --> 00:08:05.722
Let's sum up.

00:08:06.072 --> 00:08:09.153
Calling a class creates an empty object,

00:08:09.153 --> 00:08:11.232
then runs `__init__` on it.

00:08:11.582 --> 00:08:15.479
`self` is the object being worked on.

00:08:15.479 --> 00:08:19.902
`c.get_area()` means `Circle.get_area(c)`.

00:08:20.252 --> 00:08:23.062
Instance variables live in the object.

00:08:23.062 --> 00:08:25.576
Local variables live in the frame,

00:08:25.576 --> 00:08:27.572
and vanish when it returns.

00:08:27.922 --> 00:08:29.416
Here's one to try.

00:08:29.416 --> 00:08:34.229
A `Square` class should give an area of 9 for a side of 3,

00:08:34.229 --> 00:08:37.051
but it raises an `AttributeError`.

00:08:37.051 --> 00:08:39.872
Its `__init__` says `side = side`.

00:08:39.872 --> 00:08:42.362
Explain the error, and fix it.

00:08:42.912 --> 00:08:45.902
In part 3, we'll build a bigger class, a TV,

00:08:45.902 --> 00:08:49.912
and see what happens when you pass an object to a function.

